.TH XD\-VBOXDND 1 2026-10-22 XeonDesktop "XeonDesktop Manual"
.SH NAME
xd\-vboxdnd \- VirtualBox drag and drop for a XeonDesktop guest
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY xd\-vboxdnd
.OP \-\-fake\-host SCRIPT
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xd\-vboxdnd
carries drags between a VirtualBox host and a XeonDesktop guest, which
VirtualBox's own
.B VBoxClient \-\-draganddrop
refuses to do under Wayland.
.BR xd\-compositor (1)
starts it inside a VirtualBox guest (when
.I /dev/vboxuser
is there); it talks to the host's drag and drop service through that
device, and to the compositor through XeonDesktop's private drag relay
protocol.
.PP
Files (or text) dragged from the host onto the VM's window are received
into
.IR "$TMPDIR/VirtualBox Dropped Files/<time>/" ,
as VirtualBox's own guest does, then dropped on what is under the pointer.
A drag started in the guest and carried out of the VM's window is reported
to the host, and when it is dropped there its data is sent (files: with
everything they hold; symbolic links are left out). Either way it is a
copy.
.PP
The VM's
.B Devices > Drag and Drop
setting decides which ways work: Host To Guest, Guest To Host, or
Bidirectional.
.PP
If drops keep failing at once with "Can't drop data to guest", power the
VM off and start it again: VirtualBox waits forever for a transfer a
guest never finished, and refuses every drop after it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/xd-vboxdnd.trace
Every call to the host and what the drop target here said, timestamped
(up to 1 MB), for when a drag goes wrong.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-fake\-host SCRIPT
Talk to a scripted host instead of VirtualBox's, for tests; what the guest
answers is logged to
.IR SCRIPT .log
(src/vboxdnd/fakehost.c lists the script's steps).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xd\-compositor (1)
